        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What kills moss on asphalt the fastest?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Diluted household bleach and soap-based moss killers both work within hours. Bleach, at roughly one part to four parts water, is cheap and effective but harms nearby plants. Soap-based products, made from potassium salts of fatty acids, are a gentler fast option. Either works best after scraping off as much moss as possible on a dry day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will bleach damage my blacktop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Diluted bleach used occasionally and rinsed off thoroughly is unlikely to harm asphalt in a meaningful way, though some owners notice slight lightening on already faded pavement. The bigger concern is damage to grass and plants nearby. Protect them by wetting and covering them, and steer runoff away from storm drains."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is vinegar safe for an asphalt driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
